\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We present our empirical findings in five parts: strategy-dependent compound severity (\S\ref{sec:strategy-severity}), phase-modulated amplification (\S\ref{sec:phase-amplification}), the phase-strategy crossover (\S\ref{sec:crossover}), cross-model replication (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}), and statistical validation (\S\ref{sec:stat-validation}).


\subsection{Experimental Overview}
\label{sec:exp-overview}

We evaluate compound sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B using a 50-problem stratified subset of MATH-500 (\S\ref{sec:dataset}). For each problem, we measure perplexity under four conditions---dense, attention-only pruning, KV-only eviction, and compound (both)---at sparsity levels $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$. We compare importance-based and random eviction strategies (\S\ref{sec:sparsity-config}), and segment each generated sequence into high-, mid-, and low-sparsity reasoning phases via quantile-based attention sparsity partitioning (\S\ref{sec:phase-seg}). The interaction index (II, Eq.~\ref{eq:ii}) quantifies compound super-additivity, and the severity gradient ($G$, Eq.~\ref{eq:gradient}) captures phase-dependent amplification. Models are evaluated in \texttt{bfloat16} precision (\S\ref{sec:experimental-setup}). Full experimental details appear therein.


\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Severity (C1)}
\label{sec:strategy-severity}

The aggregate interaction index reveals a stark dependence on eviction strategy. Table~\ref{tab:aggregate-ii} reports the problem-level mean II across all 50 problems for each strategy and sparsity level. Under importance-based eviction, compound sparsity produces substantial super-additive degradation that grows with sparsity: mean II increases from \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s015} at $s{=}0.15$ to \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s030} at $s{=}0.30$. Under random eviction, compound interaction shows no systematic phase modulation across sparsity levels, with mean II near \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: mean_II_range}.

