\end{figure}

The replication on a second model with distinct training methodology suggests that the phase-modulated severity gradient is not an artifact of DeepSeek's distillation-induced phase structure, but rather an emergent property of how compound sparsity interacts with attention dynamics in Transformer-based reasoning. We note that both models share the same backbone architecture; generalization to other architectures (e.g., LLaMA, Mistral) remains an open question (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).


\subsection{Statistical Validation}
\label{sec:stat-validation}

All significance tests use the nonparametric bootstrap with $B = 10{,}000$ resamples. Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap} presents confidence intervals for the severity gradient under both paired and unpaired analyses.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Bootstrap confidence intervals ($B{=}10{,}000$) for the severity gradient. Paired: same problem matched across strategies, controlling for problem-level variance. Unpaired: per-strategy gradient CI. All tests reject $H_0{:}\, G = 1$ at $p < 0.0001$.}
\label{tab:bootstrap}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & Paired $G$ & Paired 95\% CI & Unpaired $G$ & Unpaired 95\% CI \\
\midrule
