\subsection{Discrimination Collapse in Importance Scoring}
\label{sec:discrimination-collapse}

The KV importance score (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance}) aggregates attention mass across all surviving heads. When pruning increases the attention entropy of these heads, the cumulative importance scores become more uniform: the gap between the most-attended and least-attended positions shrinks. We hypothesize that this uniformity degrades the \emph{discriminative power} of importance-based eviction---the ability to reliably distinguish positions critical for the model's computation from those that can be safely removed.

When the importance distribution has low entropy (a few positions dominate), thresholding the bottom-$s$ fraction removes genuinely unimportant positions. When the distribution is near-uniform, the same thresholding becomes noise-sensitive: small perturbations change which positions cross the eviction boundary. In the limit of perfectly uniform importance scores, importance-based eviction degenerates into an arbitrary selection functionally equivalent to biased random eviction.

